  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry weather continues under high pressure\n\nA broad surface high will continue to dominate over the area \ninto Saturday, while ridging strengthens aloft, leading to dry \nconditions, light winds and above average temperatures each \nafternoon. Given the dry nature of the air mass mixing down to \nthe surface, dew points were lowered the next few afternoons \nfrom NBM. Overnight lows were also lowered on average by 2-4 \ndegrees tonight, especially in the lower elevations with another\nnight of efficient radiational cooling. Any patchy fog \novernight and early tomorrow morning looks to be confined pretty\nmuch right along the rivers. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Some rain possible late Sun-Sun night\n\nThe broad surface high and ridge aloft both shift east during \nthe day Saturday setting up a shift in the weather pattern \nSunday into Monday. A surface low developing near the northern \nBahamas is expected to lift north Sunday-Sunday night while a \ncold front gradually approaches from the west. There is some \nuncertainty with the track of the low (which is being monitored \nby NHC for tropical or subtropical development), as the frontal \nboundary approaching from the west has trended a little bit \nslower from 24 hours ago, which could allow the low to track a \nbit closer to the coast. The GFS in particular has the low \ntracking noticeably farther west as it lifts northward tracking \ninland over the mid-Atlatnic vs. the Euro and Canadian which \nkeep this low and much of the moisture with it mainly to the \neast. There is a subtle different aloft as the GFS is a bit more\namplified with a 500mb trough and southern branch feature over \nthe central and northern Plains associated with the approaching \nfront. So this combined with a slightly slower front would allow\nfor that shift west. Interestingly, the Euro ensemble is in \nmore of the GFS's camp with the coastal low with a more western \ntrack. \n\nPutting it all together, right now the eastern third of the CWA\nhas the best chance of seeing some rain later Sunday-Sunday \nnight, while little in the way of moisture will be with the \nfront pushing east. The middle third of the CWA may wind up \nlargely dry between the two features. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Mixed precip possible around Thanksgiving Day\n\nSignals remain and seem to be increasing somewhat for mixed \nprecip to slide into the area from the south Wednesday or \nThursday (Thanksgiving). A surface low develops either off FL or\nsomewhere in the Deep South and rides north into the mid- \nAtlantic. All the ensembles have a 20-50pct chc of having \n>=0.25\" of liquid fall during that period (Wed-Thurs) over PA, \nesp SE PA. Wet bulb temps and 8H temps from several models are \n<=32F and 0C, respectively - with colder air being pulled in \nfrom the west. NBM mean PoPs have increased to nearly 50pct for \n0.01\" of precip. We continue to mention a the chance of \nrain/snow for the Alleghenies, with straight snow possible for \nWednesday night there and along the northern Tier of PA. A mix \nof rain/snow is also possible for the rest of the CWA with areas\nsouth and east of I-99 and I-80 changing back over to rain \nThursday afternoon.",
